WEBVTT

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.720
Several days have passed since
the end of the culture festival.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.180
The ardent atmosphere that had been
prevalent throughout the school

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.640
has now completely grown cold.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.350
Moreover...

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.980
...the bitterness of the chill
was growing worse.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.240
We need to hurry and get
our Cubs ready for winter.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.740
You're right. There's no telling
how much that will cost.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.120
For now, I should be thrifty.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.160
Me, too.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.960
What do you have?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.760
Coffee.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.110
Well, it <i>has </i>been a good while
since I made it.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.700
They say coffee tastes different
depending on who made it.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.330
So what's in yours?

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.040
Barley tea.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.620
Tastes a lot like coffee substitute.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.000
I'm trying to go without vending
machine coffee for a while.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.710
Same here.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.930
I asked someone in class where you were,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.430
and you really do eat lunch
down here, huh?

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.270
Mm-hmm. What brings you down?

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.780
Well, our shop just put in an espresso
machine for our café space.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.450
I wanted to treat you two, so if you'd
like, please come by sometime.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.240
My papa said he wanted to thank you for
what you did during the culture festival.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.460
How does today sound?

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.010
Episode 8

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.010
Super Cub

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.010
Shii's Place

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.050
I'd be delighted!

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.820
Sorry to keep you.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.370
Wow, that's an Alex Moulton, isn't it?

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.910
It has a truss frame with
a rubber cone suspension.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.710
That isn't a Japanese-made Moulton,
that's the British AM series.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.130
A "Castlebuilt," right?

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:23.180
Papa insisted that I have this one,
but I've rather come to like it.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.470
Want me to pull you along
until we get to your place?

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.560
Huh?

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.690
I've seen people go merrily right
into a ditch while doing that.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.900
I-I'll just go on ahead.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.900
Please follow me.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.350
This is it. This is my shop, "Beurre."

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.770
It means "butter" in French.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:23.240
This place is the only one around
that makes decent German bread.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.990
The name is French,
but the bread is German?

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.200
And at the culture festival,
she was a barista at an Italian bar.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.660
Call them transnational,
I guess--or scattershot.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.960
Thanks for waiting. Come on in.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:40.790
I'm home!

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:09.490
What is this? It's like
there's no cohesiveness.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:15.250
A British sandwich stand and American
diner inside a Tyrolean-style building.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:19.250
Like I said, transnational
and scattershot.

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:21.590
Is that it?

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.000
Yes. It's a used one,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.670
but after using the one at the culture
festival, I just had to have one.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:30.180
I bought it with my savings.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.220
Hey, Reiko-<i>san</i>. Welcome.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.770
Hello.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.350
Is that girl Koguma-<i>san</i>?

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:40.600
Mm-hmm.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.070
Thank you so much for coming over.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.450
My daughter told me all about
the café at the culture festival

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.450
and I was hoping I could meet you.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:02.080
We're here to enjoy
some free coffee today.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.880
Yes, by all means, enjoy some of
the coffee I'm so proud of.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.380
My daughter can't bring herself
to tell me it's any good, though.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.180
I'm going to let Reiko-<i>chan </i>and
Koguma-<i>san </i>have <i>my </i>coffee.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.770
Papa, we don't want your American coffee.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.730
We'll take both, please.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.730
Reiko and I can take one
of each and then share.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:26.940
Thank you very much.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.070
Thanks for waiting.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.620
This is a medium roast Hawaiian Kona.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.250
Thank you very much.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.630
Here, Reiko-<i>chan</i>.
One dark roast Kalosi Toraja.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.210
Thanks.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.180
Oh... Enjoy yourselves.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.050
Welcome!

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:56.850
Well, if you'll excuse me...

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:22.960
It really is different from
what I make at home.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:33.630
It's a little bitter...
but still delicious.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.780
Among bikers, good cafés are essential.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.530
So much so that the expression
"café racer" exists.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.990
The coffee and tea you drink
in these cafés keeps you warm

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.710
and acts as a stimulant
to replenish your energy.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.330
Mm-hmm...

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.470
Thank you very much.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.270
Are you actually able to run a business
opening a shop out here?

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:34.360
I got my fill of "business"
working at a company in Tokyo.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.410
If I can make a living baking my favorite
bread for the summer cottagers,

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.910
I figured that would be all I needed...

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.330
...but apparently, it's still not
enough to let me settle down.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:50.250
That's why you should hurry up
and let me take over the business.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.300
We can quit baking your nasty rye bread

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.090
and start selling paninis
and bruschettas instead!

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:59.760
You think those Italian clowns know
anything about baking or savoring bread?

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.970
I know that people who want to
have yummy food and enjoy life

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:06.270
don't eat German bread!

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:09.310
Can I get you a refill?

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:10.690
I'll take one.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.440
Me, too.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:19.240
Thank you for that.
Your coffee was delicious.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:22.740
I'm glad. Please come again, okay?

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.540
Mm-hmm, I'll be back for more.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:30.500
Hmm? Is something the matter?

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.800
It's nothing. Thank you for the coffee.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.720
Well, see you later.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.390
Be sure to come by again, okay?

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:13.500
We need to find some way
to get ready for winter, already.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.130
I don't think it's going to be that easy
finding something better than these.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.550
Clothing, Appliances

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.940
Do you remember what
we're here looking for?

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:30.540
I can never settle for anything that
isn't the best there is for myself.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.580
Whenever I come across
something like that,

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:37.880
it always leaps into my field of vision
and speaks to me, saying,

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.460
"Take me home with you!"

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.010
Mm-hmm, okay.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:55.770
Ah, a Trangia mess tin.
It's like a miniature mess kit.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:13:00.230
It not only cooks rice, you can also boil,
steam, grill, and deep fry with it.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.490
It's a do-everything cooking utensil.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.660
Okay then, what's it gonna be?

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.290
Thank you very much.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.590
Koguma-<i>san</i>, do you remember
what we're here looking for?

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:31.890
I came to buy this bento box.
I just decided.

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:38.980
What should we do? Go check out
Up Garage, just to be sure?

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:42.730
Hmm, but we just barely went there.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.950
Give me your smartphone.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.080
What? What is it?

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:00.630
Handlebar covers.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:01.500
Huh?

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:03.920
Yeah, handlebar covers.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.470
I read somewhere that people

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:09.890
who have to ride motorbikes for work
during the winter say they're the best.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:11.680
If you don't want to change
your work gloves,

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:15.430
then you can cover your handle
grips themselves with them.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:18.020
Huh? No way! They're so uncool!

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:21.270
A Cub with handlebar covers
looks like an old fogey's Cub!

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:25.150
There's no way I'm putting
them on <i>my </i>Hunter Cub.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:29.780
I'm buying some.
These are within my price range.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:08.200
Good.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:39.560
Ugh.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:13.820
Purchase

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.660
Welcome!

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:43.000
Reiko-<i>chan</i>! Koguma-<i>san</i>! You stopped by!

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.290
Ah, welcome.

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:46.790
Hello.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.220
With those handlebar covers,
my work gloves are good enough.

00:16:54.220 --> 00:16:57.010
They create a windbreak around our hands.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:01.060
It's such a simple design,
but more effective than I imagined.

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:04.270
The wind doesn't come up
our jacket sleeves.

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:07.860
That helps alleviate the cold
on our upper bodies.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:11.190
So cold-proofing is really more
like wind-proofing, isn't it?

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:14.150
Then again, I'm sure it won't be enough.

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:17.990
Well, here's to clearing
the first phase, anyhow.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:21.450
Shii-<i>chan</i>, can I get a refill?

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:22.910
Shii-<i>chan</i>?

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:24.290
Huh? Oh...

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:29.040
I'm sorry. Listen, maybe
you should get going now.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.250
Huh?

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:33.380
This is about the time when we get
a lot of customers coming in.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:35.800
I really am sorry.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:38.140
Oh, I see.

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.390
Just a moment.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:45.310
I'd like you both to sample
my <i>landbrot </i>before you go.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:49.610
"<i>Landbrot</i>"? Isn't that the rye bread
that German soldiers choked down?

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:53.860
This is much more palatable than
the <i>komisbrot </i>the soldiers had.

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:54.700
I'll have some!

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.070
But this is when we get
swarmed with customers!

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:59.740
Huh? I'm sure we'll be fine.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.240
Oh, I'm telling you, no!

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:08.880
Okay, well...

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.960
Now, now, where's the harm,
where's the harm?

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:13.710
I'll try some of that rye bread.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:16.470
That's more like it.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:17.550
Papa!

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:19.090
Oh, Reiko-<i>chan</i>!

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:21.760
If it gets crowded,
we'll leave right away.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:26.810
Unless... there's some <i>other </i>reason
you're trying to chase us out of here?

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:29.100
No, not particularly...

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.880
I'm home!

00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:59.470
Welcome back.

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:01.390
Shii's friends are here.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:03.640
Friends?

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:06.140
Oh, Reiko-<i>san</i>, nice to see you.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:07.350
Hello.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:11.230
And also, look, it's Koguma-<i>san</i>,
from the culture festival.

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:14.320
Huh? Oh!

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:18.360
You're the one? My daughter has
told me so much about you!

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.950
It's a pleasure to meet you, Koguma-<i>san</i>.

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:26.040
I'm Shii's mother.

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:29.290
Hello...

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:31.960
Ooh, I've been hoping to meet you!

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.760
Here you go.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:44.890
Koguma-<i>san</i>?

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:46.680
I'm fine.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.270
If you need a refill, just let me know.

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.270
Oh, thank you.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:02.610
A father who's obsessed with Germany
and an America-loving mother...

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:06.490
The transition in the floor there
is like the Atlantic Ocean.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:11.870
The sandwich counter is British, right?

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:16.040
That's probably a middle ground
that the two compromised on.

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:17.710
Hmm...

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:24.470
And a tiny sliver of Italy.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:31.100
It might be fun to come by
and see just how big it becomes.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:34.350
Well, as long as she's riding
a British-made Moulton,

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:36.230
there's plenty of room.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:58.340
You look different than in the
classroom when you're wearing that.

00:20:58.340 --> 00:21:00.710
Huh? Oh...

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:06.720
Please come by again, okay?

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.310
Mm-hmm.

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:09.430
Reiko-<i>chan</i>, you, too.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.770
I'll stop by in the morning. Until then.

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.140
Mm-hmm.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.960
We were feeling the tail end of autumn.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.470
Our handlebar covers were outstanding,

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:49.760
but we'll need some more basic
cold-weather measures going forward.

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:56.480
I figured that there was no
future in getting café coffee

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:02.030
when I can make it at home and
it costs as much as a full tank of gas.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:12.580
But that is typically speaking.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:19.750
Supposing I wanted to
indulge myself a little,

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:24.050
and absolutely have to have
a cup to warm myself up...

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:30.640
...it wouldn't be such a bad thing
to stop by that shop... would it?

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:15.970
Episode 9

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:15.970
Super Cub

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:15.970
In Ice

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:15.950
Episode 9: "In Ice."
